The frozen `development-v1` run qualifies a small native transition-storage path
and an explicit evaluation dispatcher. All 44 case/arm tasks passed independent
checks of actual subprocess output. Native memory reduced action executions from
27 to 16; exact JSON retrieval and full-condition templates also used 16. Native
memory was slightly slower in total process time. These results support an
exact-lookup mechanism, with no demonstrated learned world-model advantage.

This is one constructed development sequence in a closed, three-action integer
service. It is not a benchmark of repository repair, cross-family transfer,
token savings, or a production-integrated world model. Full-scope E05 remains
open. No providers or models were called and no production source was changed.

`protocol.json` was persisted before execution; the run preserves its hash and
the hashes of the executable evaluation code in `prerun_freeze.json`. The common
seed executed three actions, then published its successful transition. All four
arms received byte-identical copies of that history and the same current task
evidence. No-memory deliberately does not consult the previously selected
action. The exact JSON control compares the entire query. The template control
compares the entire source/environment/state/policy condition tuple; it has no
generalized rule or extra label.

Each subsequent case/arm started in a new interpreter, reopened its isolated
SQLite/block store, and executed its selected action in another subprocess.
The action process received the action and input integer; it did not receive
the expected output. The separate consumer compared actual output to the task's
target. Successful later observations were persisted for the next process. No
stored output replaced current execution or validation.

| Frozen development sequence, 11 tasks per arm | Native memory | No memory | Exact JSON | Condition template |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Correct tasks | 11 | 11 | 11 | 11 |
| Actual action subprocesses | 16 | 27 | 16 | 16 |
| Actions including common seed | 19 | 30 | 19 | 19 |
| Total process seconds | 8.947150 | 8.739228 | 8.352039 | 8.547626 |
| Process seconds including common seed | 10.126975 | 9.919054 | 9.531865 | 9.727451 |
| Action subprocess seconds | 0.327071 | 0.486259 | 0.287187 | 0.312338 |
| Retrieval/admission seconds | 0.223937 | 0.000010 | 0.013139 | 0.009495 |
| Final persistent store bytes | 261587 | 273938 | 260706 | 260706 |

The shared seed cost 1.179825 process seconds, including 0.053549 action seconds
and 0.200312 publication seconds. The 40.7% reduction in later action calls
(36.7% charging the seed to every arm) is a descriptive result of this chosen
sequence. All arms import the same native packages and publish the same kind of
evidence, so the timings include substantial common import/storage overhead.
They are not population estimates or optimized standalone baseline timings.
The native arm took 0.207921 more total process seconds than no-memory in this
single run. All 78 executed action attempts, including ordinary failed search
attempts, have preserved stdout, stderr, return code and elapsed time.

Changed state, changed source and changed declared environment were isolated
separately: each changed only its corresponding query field and query CID.
Native admission returned `stale` and native candidate binding rejected the old
candidate in all three cases. Actual fallback search succeeded with two action
calls. Each immediate repeat then used its newly stored observation in one
call. An `inferred_untrusted` observation produced `abstain`, triggered three
actual search calls, and its fresh observed replacement supported the next
process. This is conservative invalidation of declared bindings; it does not
establish complete environmental dependency discovery.

The unmodified profile is `.worktrees/semantic-addressed-world-model-r2`, clean
at commit `40451b52c133b943818515a86e0deb95d9fab812`. Imports used its normal
accelerate/datasets/kit package roots with supported minimal-import flags. The
actual subprocess profile records Python, platform, executable hash and 166
imported source-module paths/hashes. Important source pins are:

| Native source | SHA-256 |
| --- | --- |
| `program_transition.py` | `fd6cacb79b4500b1481b1173cab8d6455c73dce45e3075a3f5263cc73daaad91` |
| `graph_history.py` | `3fbfe98f47218625476449a8036d7bd8ef6898970c92d925cce294965e9f020a` |
| `coordination_storage.py` | `6c5adeeee8fb5be606392636f2b2ccfcff660ba6323271d35bf1e34022fdc8bb` |
| `program_world_adapters.py` | `ac947158c66729fd63333cfebf82886a887ac7683e9a515a1f293357104e7788` |

The native path is `observe_program_transition` →
`ProgramTransitionLog.append_transition_receipt` →
`DurableCoordinationStore` → new-process verified query/candidate/observation
retrieval → `admit_program_transition` and `bind_candidate_to_query`. Native
store bytes and record identities are content-verified. The `observed` status
is a declaration by this local evaluation producer; these records carry no
runner signature or cryptographic execution proof. The producer, retrieval
index, catalog search, dispatcher and output checker in
`evaluation_consumer.py` are newly written evaluation integration. No learned
predictor, automatic semantic updater or production planner was exercised.

The native `ExecutionTransitionCompiler` emitted unadmitted proposals throughout.
A post-run audit also found that all 11 compilations put the query CID into
both `candidate_cid` and `observation_cid`, rather than preserving their distinct
record identities. `DatasetsProgramWorldAdapter.cite_transition` searches for
`query_cid` before the other identity fields. The frozen consumer selected
actions using the separately retrieved typed candidate, never those compiler
fields. Thus the successful action sequence does not qualify compiler-mediated
dispatch. The exact receipts and expected/actual IDs are preserved in
`runs/development-v1/compiler_identity_audit.json`. Any subsequent source fix and
compiler-consuming run must use a separately versioned isolated profile.

`validate.py` independently checked the frozen raw outputs, process counts,
fresh-process provenance, exact shared evidence, declared-binding changes,
source hashes and preregistered outcomes: **893/893 checks passed**. That audit
does not certify the compiler identity fields; their separate audit records
11/11 candidate and 11/11 observation identity mismatches. There were no failed
evaluation-process iterations to omit. Both adverse timing and compiler
findings are retained.

Reproduce in a new, unused run directory:

```sh
.venv/bin/python papers/revisions/neurosymbolic_system_scope_20260914/evals/world_memory_development/run.py --run-id development-next
.venv/bin/python papers/revisions/neurosymbolic_system_scope_20260914/evals/world_memory_development/validate.py --run-id development-next
.venv/bin/python papers/revisions/neurosymbolic_system_scope_20260914/evals/world_memory_development/audit_compiler_identity.py --run-id development-next
```

Primary evidence: `runs/development-v1/summary.json`, `execution_log.json`,
`validation.json`, `compiler_identity_audit.json`, each process's `result.json`,
raw action outputs, and the actual local `seed_store/` and `stores/` artifacts.
